  "order": 11,
  "title": "The flat keys in order",
  "summary": "Flats are added in the reverse order of sharps.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Order of flats",
      "body": "Flats are added in the opposite order to sharps: B♭ E♭ A♭ D♭ G♭ C♭ F♭. F major has the first one, B♭ major the first two, E♭ major the first three. This is the **order of flats**.\n\n**Why it matters:** Band charts are full of flat keys, especially with horns. Knowing the order tells you exactly which black keys a flat key uses.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        70,
        63,
        68,
        61,
        66
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "4th: D♭",
        "63": "2nd: E♭",
        "66": "5th: G♭",
        "68": "3rd: A♭",
        "70": "1st: B♭"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "From the flats to the key",
      "body": "With two or more flats, the second-to-last flat names the key. Three flats are B♭ E♭ A♭, and the second-to-last is E♭, so three flats means E♭ major. One flat is the odd one out: F major.\n\n**Why it matters:** with this trick you can read any flat key signature in a second.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        63,
        65,
        67,
        68,
        70,
        72,
        74,
        75
      ],
      "labels": {
        "63": "E♭",
        "65": "F",
        "67": "G",
        "68": "A♭",
        "70": "B♭",
        "72": "C",
        "74": "D",
        "75": "E♭"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "The flat keys",
      "body": "F (1♭), B♭ (2♭), E♭ (3♭), A♭ (4♭) and D♭ (5♭).",
